-- title:
--   Ordinary corner: supersingular residues versus finite p-torsion
-- statement:
--   Fix an odd prime $p$ (hypothesis `hp2`) and a level $M$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ which, by `hHp`, contains every unit whose image under reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is $1$. Write $J_H(M)$ for [`ModularCurve.JH M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L127), the group of degree-zero divisor classes of the function field $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-realisation `xHFunctionFieldBar M H`, and $H' =$ `infSubgroup p M H hpM`, the image of $H$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$. A set $S$ of naturals is fixed, together with the hypothesis `hin`, which asserts the two clauses of `HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`: the predicate `HeckeInputsHAlong (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) M H ℓ` holds for every prime $\ell$, and every $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ is realised by an automorphism of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ satisfying `IsDiamondAutHBar M H d`.
--
--   Hecke data. A commutative ring $\mathbb{T}$, a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra, acts on the Tate module [`TateModule p (JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) (whose elements are sequences $(x_n)$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p\,x_{n+1} = x_n$) compatibly with the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-action; `hfaith` says the action is faithful. A map `op` from the generators [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (symbols $T_\ell$, $U_q$, $\langle d\rangle$) to $\mathbb{T}$ is given, with `hop` asserting that `op g` acts on the Tate module as [`ModularCurve.tateGenOpH M H S p g`](def/ModularCurve_XHOperators.html#L99), and `hgen` asserting that the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-subalgebra generated by the range of `op` is all of $\mathbb{T}$. Furthermore `S'` is an [`IharaLemma.IdempotentSplitting`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L7) of $\mathbb{T}$: finitely many complete orthogonal idempotents `S'.e i`, maximal ideals `S'.𝔪 i` exhausting the maximal ideals of $\mathbb{T}$, with `S'.e i ∈ S'.𝔪 j` exactly when $i \neq j$. An index $i_0$ is fixed with `hord`: $\mathrm{op}(U_p) \notin$ `S'.𝔪 i₀`, the ordinarity condition at the chosen corner.
--
--   Arithmetic of the place. `Pl` is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `hPl`: $p$ lies in its nonunits, and its residue field is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$. A $\mathbb{T}$-submodule `P0` of the Tate module is given, characterised by `hP0`: $x \in$ `P0` if and only if $x$ lies in [`IharaLemma.cornerSubmodule (S'.e i₀)`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L46), the range of multiplication by the idempotent `S'.e i₀`, and for every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` the Galois action `JH.tateGaloisRep M H p σ` sends $x$ to $\chi_{\mathrm{cyc}}(\sigma)\,x$, the cyclotomic character at $p$.
--
--   Differentials in characteristic $p$. $K$ is an algebraically closed $(\mathbb{Z}/p)$-algebra, and $\tau$ is a ring homomorphism from $\mathbb{T}$ to the $K$-endomorphisms of [`ModularCurve.ssPolarDifferentials K (CohCarrier.GammaH (M/p) H') p`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L35), the space of differentials of `qExpFunctionFieldC K (GammaH (M/p) H')` over $K$ which are regular away from the supersingular places `ssPlacesQExp` and have at most simple poles there. The hypothesis `hτ` says that for every generator $g$ the endomorphism $\tau(\mathrm{op}(g))$ acts on underlying differentials as [`ModularCurve.genDiffModL K p M H hpM S g`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L266), i.e. as `heckeDiffModLH` for $T_\ell$ and for $U_q$ with $q \neq p$, as the Frobenius pushforward `frobPushDiffModL` for $U_p$, and as `diamondDiffModLH` for a diamond symbol.
--
--   Integral model and Néron objects. With `hj` the hypothesis that `jqModC ℚ` lies in `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`, the data $\mathfrak{X}$ is an `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` — a Deligne–Rapoport style integral model of $X_H(M)$ over `R p` together with its curve model `𝔛.Meta` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the comparison `𝔛.eeta`, the Atkin–Lehner isomorphism `𝔛.w`, the degeneracy morphisms `𝔛.π`, `𝔛.πw`, the diamond automorphisms `𝔛.dia0`, the fibre curve model `𝔛.Mfib` and its component maps. Next, $\Lambda$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData p M H hpM Pl` (a section `Λ.σA` over `Pl`, a scheme `Λ.X` with structure morphism `Λ.f`, a relative group law `Λ.L`, and bijections `Λ.pts`, `Λ.ptsSp` identifying $J_{H'}(M/p)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ and the degree-zero Picard group of the residue fibre with sections of `Λ.f`), and `hrepΛ` asserts that the designation built from `Λ.X`, `Λ.f` and the unit section represents the relative sub-Picard functor cut out by `algEquivZeroCut` for the model at level `ΓN p M H hpM` with section `schemeHomOverComp 𝔛.εinf 𝔛.π`. Then $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`: a smooth separated group object `O.G` over `base p` with relative group law `O.L`, a bijection `O.pts` from $J_H(M)$ to sections over the generic point, Hecke endomorphisms `O.hecke`, degeneracy homomorphisms `O.degPts`, a toric rank `O.toricRank`, and the subgroups `O.toricPts m` and `O.finPts m` of $J_H(M)$; by definition `O.finPts m` is generated by those $m$-torsion classes $x$ for which the predicate `ExtendsToPlace` holds of `Pl`, `Λ.σA` and the section `O.pts x`. The hypotheses `hD`, `hDQ` and `hsep` state that the designation attached to $O$ represents the `algEquivZeroCut` sub-Picard functor over `R p` and over its base change to $\mathbb{Q}$, and that the generic base change is separated.
--
--   Abel–Jacobi data (summarised here). A section `ajQ` over the generic base change, a comparison morphism `kQ` between the two pullbacks, a morphism `ajbar : 𝔛.Meta.C ⟶ O.G` and a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point `εbar` of `𝔛.Meta.C` are given, subject to: `hpoinc` (an isomorphism between the Poincaré bundle of `hDQ` and the base change of that of `hD`), `hajQε` (the cusp section composed with `ajQ` is the zero section), `hajQ` (for every field, every point of $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Q}$ over it and every section $x$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by `ajQ` is isomorphic to the tensor product of the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ with the ideal module of that of the cusp section — the Abel–Jacobi normalisation), `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂` (compatibility of `kQ` with both projections), `hajbar` and `hajbar_over` (the definition of `ajbar` as `𝔛.eeta` followed by `kQ`, `ajQ` and the first projection, and its compatibility with the structure morphisms), `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj` (that `εbar` is the distinguished section and is carried to the unit), `hpts_law` (that `O.pts` is additive for the relative group law supplied by `hD`), and `hAJ` (for any two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$, $s$ of `𝔛.Meta.C` with $s$ the distinguished one, there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $(x) - (s)$ under `𝔛.Meta.pointEquivPlace` whose class has `O.pts` equal to $x$ followed by `ajbar`).
--
--   $p$-divisible group data. $R$ is a commutative domain and henselian local ring with algebraically closed residue field, faithfully embedded in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, with `hRA` (its image lies in `Pl`), `hRdvr` (it is a discrete valuation ring), `hRirr` ($p$ is irreducible in it), `hRfix` (an automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ lies in the inertia subgroup of `Pl` exactly when it fixes $R$ pointwise) and `hRmax` (every element of `Pl` fixed by inertia comes from $R$). Over $R$ a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ of height $h$ is given (levels `𝒢.level v` finite free Hopf algebras of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transition maps), together with an injective additive map $\Delta$ from $\mathcal{G}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $J_H(M)$ satisfying: `hΔlev` ($\Delta$ identifies the level-$v$ points with `O.finPts (p ^ v)`), `hΔgal` (equivariance for automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ lifting to $R$-automorphisms), and `hΔhecke` (each generator $g$ is realised by a system of coalgebra endomorphisms of the levels commuting with the transition maps and inducing `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$). A second $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ over $R$ of height $h_B$ with $h_B = 2h'$, and level maps $\psi$ from $\mathcal{B}$ to $\mathcal{G}$, are given with `hhB` ($h =$ `O.toricRank` $+\, h_B$), `hψt` (compatibility with transitions), `hψker` (a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$ dies in $\mathcal{B}$ exactly when its $\Delta$-image lies in `O.toricPts (p ^ v)`), `hψsurj` (surjectivity on points), `hψred` (if the $\mathcal{B}$-image of a point is congruent to the counit with respect to the valuation of `Pl`, so is the point itself), and `hperiod` (for $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup, if $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point equals $\sigma z - z$ for some $p^v$-torsion class $z$, then its $\mathcal{B}$-image is congruent to the counit). Finally a ring homomorphism `ρh : R p →+* R` and morphisms `ι v : Spec (𝒢.level v) ⟶ O.G` are given, subject to `hρh` (compatibility with the maps into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$), `hιbase` (each `ι v` lies over `ρh`), `hιcl` (the induced morphism is a closed immersion), `hιp` (`ι v` factors through the kernel of multiplication by $p^v$), `hιpts` (`ι v` computes `O.pts` of the $\Delta$-image of a point), `hιmul` (`ι v` is multiplicative on points with values in any $R$-algebra), `hιt` (compatibility with the transition maps), `hιhecke` (compatibility with the Hecke endomorphisms `O.hecke`, again inducing `genOpH` through $\Delta$), and `hιfin` (the morphism `jv` into the pullback describing the $p^v$-torsion over $R$ is both an open and a closed immersion, and its image contains every point lying over the closed point of $R$).
--
--   Atkin–Lehner and reduction data. A semilinear automorphism `wgen` of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is given with `hwgen`: it implements the action of `𝔛.w` on places through `𝔛.Meta.pointEquivPlace`. A $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism $\theta$ of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` is given with `hθ`: whenever $f$ at level $M$ and $u$ at level $M/p$ have the same Laurent expansion, $\theta(f)$ has expansion `qExpand (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) p u`, that is $u(q^p)$; and `hwθ`: `wgen = SemilinearAut.ofAlgAut θ`. A ring homomorphism `ρ : R p →+* Pl` with `hρ` compatible with the map to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is given, and `hσA`: `Λ.σA = Spec.map ρ`. The hypothesis `hsp` (its many binders summarised here) states, for each $i \in \{0,1\}$, that given two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $y_1$, $y_2$ of `𝔛.Meta.C` with lifts $u_1$, $u_2$ to sections of the integral model over $\operatorname{Spec} Pl$ whose images lie in `𝔛.smoothLocus`, compatible residue-field points $u_{\kappa 1}$, $u_{\kappa 2}$ of the fibre, closed points $P_1$, $P_2$ of the fibre curve model reducing to them along the $i$-th component map, a degree-zero divisor equal to $(y_1) - (y_2)$, and an admissible gluing datum whose first component is $(P_1) - (P_2)$ when $i = 0$ and $0$ otherwise, whose second component is $(P_1) - (P_2)$ when $i = 1$ and $0$ otherwise, and whose unit component vanishes, there exists a section $s$ of `O.g` over `Λ.σA` with `O.pts` of the divisor class equal to `barPt Pl` followed by $s$, and with the reduction of $s$ corresponding under `O.ptsSp` to the class of that gluing datum in `GluedPic0`. The hypothesis `hspΛ` is the analogous reduction statement one level down: under the corresponding data, with $Q_1$, $Q_2$ reducing along `𝔛.π` or `𝔛.πw` according to $i$, and a degree-zero divisor $D_w = (Q_1) - (Q_2)$ on the fibre, there is a section $s_0$ of `Λ.f` over `Λ.σA` representing `Λ.pts (O.degPts i (Pic0.mk Dv))` whose reduction corresponds to `Pic0.mk Dw`. The hypothesis `hdia0` says that the diamond automorphisms `𝔛.dia0 e` act on closed points of the fibre curve model through `diamondActionModL` applied to [`CuspForm.gammaLift (M/p) e`](def/CuspForm_HeckeOperatorFormsGammaH.html#L36). A unit $d$ of $\mathbb{Z}/M$ is given with `hd` (its reduction equals $p$ in $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$) and `hdH` (its reduction, or the negative thereof, lies in $H'$), and $M/p$ is nonzero.
--
--   Frobenius and degeneracy data on the fibre. Additive endomorphisms $F$, $F^{-1}$, $F^*$ of the degree-zero Picard group of `JHNeronObjectAtP.Fbar p M H hpM (ResidueField Pl)` are given with `hF` ($F$ is `qExpFrobeniusPushforwardModL` at level `ΓN p M H hpM`), `hFinv` ($F$ and $F^{-1}$ are mutually inverse) and `hFstar` ($F^* = p \cdot F^{-1}$). A unit `pb` of $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ equal to $p$ is given together with the diamond operator $\delta$ acting through `diamondActionModL` applied to [`CuspForm.gammaLift (M/p) pb`](def/CuspForm_HeckeOperatorFormsGammaH.html#L36). Two degeneracy homomorphisms `αpull i : J_{H'}(M/p) →+ J_H(M)` and two morphisms `degPull i` from `Λ.f` to `O.g` are given with `hpull` (`O.pts (αpull i x)` is `Λ.pts x` followed by `degPull i`), `hpullsp` (on reductions, the pair of classes attached to `degPull i` is $(z, F^* z)$ for $i = 0$ and $(F^* z, \delta z)$ for $i = 1$), and `hpull_mul` (each `degPull i` is compatible with the two relative group laws). An additive endomorphism `Wbar` of $J_H(M)$ is given with `hWbar`: it is the action of `wgen`. The hypothesis `hUPgen` is the relation $U_p x + \overline{W} x = \alpha_1(\mathrm{degPts}_0(x))$ for all $x \in J_H(M)$.
--
--   Residues. Finally a $K$-linear map `res` from the supersingular-polar differentials to functions on the places of `qExpFunctionFieldC K (GammaH (M/p) H')` is given, with `hres`: for every supersingular place $v$, `res ω v` is a simple residue of $\omega$ at $v$ in the sense of `Place.HasSimpleResidue`; and `hres0`: `res ω v = 0` for $v$ not supersingular.
--
--   Conclusion. Let $X$ denote the image in $J_H(M)$ of the additive subgroup underlying [`IharaLemma.cornerSubmodule (S'.e i₀)`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L46) under the projection [`TateModule.proj p (JH M H) 1`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L122) of the Tate module onto its component of index $1$. Then
--   $$p^{\;\dim_K \, \operatorname{res}\bigl(\operatorname{range} \tau(S'.e\,i_0)\bigr)} \cdot \#\{x \in X : x \in O.\mathrm{finPts}\ p\} \;=\; \# X,$$
--   where the exponent is the $K$-dimension of the image under `res` of the range of the idempotent endomorphism $\tau(S'.e\,i_0)$ of the supersingular-polar differentials, the first cardinality is the `Set.ncard` of the set of elements of $X$ lying in `O.finPts p`, and the right-hand side is the `Nat.card` of $X$.
--
--   This is the counting step, in the ordinary corner cut out by the idempotent `S'.e i₀`, which measures the subgroup of $p$-torsion classes whose sections extend over the place `Pl` inside the whole corner of $J_H(M)[p]$: the index is the power of $p$ given by the dimension of the space of residues at supersingular points of the corner of the polar differentials on the curve of level $M/p$ in characteristic $p$. It is the form in which the $p$-divisible-group and reduction data of the Deligne–Rapoport model enter Ribet's level-lowering argument, and it is used by the companion statement [`ModularCurve.pow_finrank_range_corner_ssPolarDifferentials_mul_ncard_reducesToOne_eq_of_abelJacobiPin_of_representsRelSubPicLevel_raynaud_bridgePins_noKTransport_bridgePins4_of_algEquiv`](thm.html#ModularCurve.pow_finrank_range_corner_ssPolarDifferentials_mul_ncard_reducesToOne_eq_of_abelJacobiPin_of_representsRelSubPicLevel_raynaud_bridgePins_noKTransport_bridgePins4_of_algEquiv).
